{
  "case_id": "2004-03-1407",
  "document_hash": "e2ed47a5b8b3709e983b883683ea3912b6892341198a88d88e3f2181cd8854c9",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2004-03-1407",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e2ed47a5b8b3709e983b883683ea3912b6892341198a88d88e3f2181cd8854c9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e2ed47a5b8b3-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:03 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2004-03-1407",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e2ed47a5b8b3709e983b883683ea3912b6892341198a88d88e3f2181cd8854c9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e2ed47a5b8b3-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: What does the word holistic mean?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2004-03-1407",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e2ed47a5b8b3709e983b883683ea3912b6892341198a88d88e3f2181cd8854c9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e2ed47a5b8b3-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. KARLAN: That's correct, but the 10 percent tax here is designed and does, in fact, operate as a deterrent and a penalty."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2004-03-1407",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e2ed47a5b8b3709e983b883683ea3912b6892341198a88d88e3f2181cd8854c9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e2ed47a5b8b3-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Ms. Karlan, the -- as long as this money was in the plan, it was shielded from bankruptcy, and there are -- there was not unlimited access even with the penalty when it was in the plan."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. KARLAN",
      "case_id": "2004-03-1407",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e2ed47a5b8b3709e983b883683ea3912b6892341198a88d88e3f2181cd8854c9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e2ed47a5b8b3-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Anytime you deviate from the form that the Internal Revenue Service gives you, which also gives you these rights to withdraw early subject to penalty, you run the risk that your plan will then be held to be a nonqualifying plan under section 408 of the tax code."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. KARLAN",
      "case_id": "2004-03-1407",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e2ed47a5b8b3709e983b883683ea3912b6892341198a88d88e3f2181cd8854c9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e2ed47a5b8b3-0001-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "46 States offer some kind of protection. 23 States protect them without limit in bankruptcy and out. 6 of them protect them inside of bankruptcy using the State exemptions as long as the amount is reasonable and necessary for the debtor's support."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. KARLAN",
      "case_id": "2004-03-1407",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e2ed47a5b8b3709e983b883683ea3912b6892341198a88d88e3f2181cd8854c9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e2ed47a5b8b3-0001-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There's a weird anomaly in Arkansas, Your Honor, which is Arkansas law does, in fact, protect IRA's, but the Federal bankruptcy courts in Arkansas have interpreted that law only to protect the IRA up to $500 because of a provision in the Arkansas constitution, article 9, section 2, that means that you can only save up to $500."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. KARLAN",
      "case_id": "2004-03-1407",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e2ed47a5b8b3709e983b883683ea3912b6892341198a88d88e3f2181cd8854c9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e2ed47a5b8b3-0001-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, if you take, say, $55,000 and you ask what sort of annuity could you purchase when you hit age 59 and a half with that money, it will be an annuity that, I would guess -- you know, I -- I hate to do math in my head like this. I'd guess it would throw off a couple of hundred dollars a month in additional income."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. KARLAN",
      "case_id": "2004-03-1407",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e2ed47a5b8b3709e983b883683ea3912b6892341198a88d88e3f2181cd8854c9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e2ed47a5b8b3-0001-0005",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "assume clearly from what you say is that there is not only no statute, but no IRS reg or ruling to the effect that you can make your IRA terms more restrictive without jeopardizing your qualification."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2004-03-1407",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e2ed47a5b8b3709e983b883683ea3912b6892341198a88d88e3f2181cd8854c9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e2ed47a5b8b3-0001-0006",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We don't have an amicus brief here from the Government, do we?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. McKIEVER",
      "case_id": "2004-03-1407",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e2ed47a5b8b3709e983b883683ea3912b6892341198a88d88e3f2181cd8854c9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e2ed47a5b8b3-0001-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Pursuant to section 522(d)(10)(e) of the Bankruptcy Code, a debtor's right to receive a payment is not exempt unless two requirements are met. First, the right to receive the payment must be on account of illness, disability, death, age or length of service, and the right must come from a specified similar plan or contract."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice STEVENS",
      "case_id": "2004-03-1407",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e2ed47a5b8b3709e983b883683ea3912b6892341198a88d88e3f2181cd8854c9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e2ed47a5b8b3-0001-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Supposing instead of a 10 percent penalty, there was an absolute prohibition, would you agree -- on getting the money before you're 59 and a half, would then that qualify?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. McKIEVER",
      "case_id": "2004-03-1407",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e2ed47a5b8b3709e983b883683ea3912b6892341198a88d88e3f2181cd8854c9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e2ed47a5b8b3-0001-0009",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Absolutely, yes, it would."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. McKIEVER",
      "case_id": "2004-03-1407",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e2ed47a5b8b3709e983b883683ea3912b6892341198a88d88e3f2181cd8854c9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e2ed47a5b8b3-0002-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But they -- they definitely exist. In fact, in the Andersen case out of the Eighth Circuit Bankruptcy Appellate Panel, the debtor had an annuity and prior to the filing of the bankruptcy petition, she elected that she would only receive periodic payments based upon her age, and that was found to be exempt by the Bankruptcy Appellate Panel because that qualified."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2004-03-1407",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e2ed47a5b8b3709e983b883683ea3912b6892341198a88d88e3f2181cd8854c9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e2ed47a5b8b3-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Was that -- was that an IRA that she had? It was an IRA?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2004-03-1407",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e2ed47a5b8b3709e983b883683ea3912b6892341198a88d88e3f2181cd8854c9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e2ed47a5b8b3-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: What -- what if they don't repay it? What's the sanction?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. McKIEVER",
      "case_id": "2004-03-1407",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e2ed47a5b8b3709e983b883683ea3912b6892341198a88d88e3f2181cd8854c9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e2ed47a5b8b3-0002-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There is a 10 percent penalty."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2004-03-1407",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e2ed47a5b8b3709e983b883683ea3912b6892341198a88d88e3f2181cd8854c9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e2ed47a5b8b3-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 51,
        "start": 51
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The -- the first point is that there are two rights under IRA's: the right after age 59 and a half or upon death or disability or illness to withdraw the money without any kind of penalty; and there's a second little exercise, an entirely subsidiary right, which is the ability to withdraw money subject to a penalty earlier."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2004-03-1407",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e2ed47a5b8b3709e983b883683ea3912b6892341198a88d88e3f2181cd8854c9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e2ed47a5b8b3-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: In any of these other plans, can you get the money so long as you're willing to pay a penalty?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2004-03-1407",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e2ed47a5b8b3709e983b883683ea3912b6892341198a88d88e3f2181cd8854c9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e2ed47a5b8b3-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: Has any judge ever taken the view that -- adopting Justice Scalia's approach, that while you can't put the whole IRA -- exempt the whole IRA, but you can exempt 10 percent of it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Karlan",
      "case_id": "2004-03-1407",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e2ed47a5b8b3709e983b883683ea3912b6892341198a88d88e3f2181cd8854c9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e2ed47a5b8b3-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I don't know, but the New York City Police Department plan appears to work like that. You can take the money out if you pay the 10 percent penalty."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2004-03-1407",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e2ed47a5b8b3709e983b883683ea3912b6892341198a88d88e3f2181cd8854c9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e2ed47a5b8b3-0003-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The second point is a point that comes out of this Court's decision last year in the Till case where the Court said, look, you want to pick a manageable line, a line that's straightforward and familiar."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Karlan",
      "case_id": "2004-03-1407",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e2ed47a5b8b3709e983b883683ea3912b6892341198a88d88e3f2181cd8854c9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e2ed47a5b8b3-0003-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And this may have led to some of the confusion, I think, between counsel in this case, which is in the Andersen case, although Ms. Andersen was receiving money under section 408, it was not a 408 individual retirement account. It was, instead, under section 403(b), an individual retirement annuity."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
